{{!
	Body before-content: the strip that opens div#bodyContent. It is rendered on every page,
	directly beneath the page toolbar and immediately above div#contentSub, and it carries at
	most two children: the page status indicators, when the page titlebar is occupied by the
	language button, and the site tagline, on article pages only. Both children are
	conditional, so on an ordinary non-article view this is an empty container.

	bool has-buttons-in-content-top true when the language dropdown or the add-topic button
		occupies the page titlebar, which is where the indicators would otherwise be rendered;
		they move down into this strip instead. This positive section is the exact complement
		of the inverted has-buttons-in-content-top section in PageTitlebar.mustache, so
		between the two templates the Indicators partial sits on exactly one code path and
		div.mw-indicators is emitted exactly once per page. Do not invert this test and do not
		wrap the partial in a second guard: either change yields zero or two indicator
		containers.
	bool is-article core flag derived from OutputPage::isArticle(). It gates the tagline so
		that the element is emitted on content pages only, and never on special pages,
		history views or diffs.
	string msg-tagline the core tagline message, rendered in English as "From" followed by the
		site name. SkinMustache::getTemplateData() supplies it as plain text for every key
		listed in skin.json's messages array; it is a core message key, so it is deliberately
		not renamed to a notion- key and needs no entry in this skin's i18n/en.json. The
		double-brace expression below HTML-escapes it, which is correct for plain text.

	Platform DOM contract, reproduced verbatim below: neither the id nor the class on the
	tagline element may be renamed. The id is the long-standing public selector that on-wiki
	site CSS and gadgets use to reveal or restyle the tagline, which ships hidden: core's
	interface-subtitle feature hides it, and because the modern feature set switches that
	feature off, the skin re-declares the hide in its own typography stylesheet, per T335625,
	so that on-wiki overrides keep working. The class is the platform-wide print-suppression
	convention, applied by core itself elsewhere and implemented generically by one shared
	rule in the skin's print stylesheet, so this element needs no print rule of its own and
	none should be added for it. For that shared class rule to keep winning, the skin's own
	rule for this element must stay scoped to screen media: a bare id selector out-specifies
	a class selector and a media query contributes no specificity of its own, so an unscoped
	id rule would survive into print and defeat the suppression. The only name this skin owns
	in this partial is the wrapper class below, whose appearance lives in
	resources/skins.notion.styles/layouts/screen.less: it contains the floating indicators
	and spaces them from the page chrome above.
}}
<div class="notion-body-before-content">
	{{#has-buttons-in-content-top}}
		{{>Indicators}}
	{{/has-buttons-in-content-top}}

	{{#is-article}}<div id="siteSub" class="noprint">{{msg-tagline}}</div>{{/is-article}}
</div>
